WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.520
Based on the CIA's data, the country with the highest life expectancy is Monaco

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.040
with an average of 89.5 years old.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.280
In the United States, it drops to about eighty.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.060
In Canada, about 82

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.500
In the United Kingdom, around 81.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.180
Across the world, this number can fluctuate dramatically

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.890
and drop all the way down to the low 50s in certain less developed countries.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.119
But for the sake of this video,

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:24.720
let's assume your life expectancy is on the higher side of the world's average

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.100
and that you will have no incidents of health complications or tragedies,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.400
and that you will live to 82 years old.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.640
82 years on this planet as yourself.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.940
Not too bad, but that's from birth.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.880
In terms of your remaining time in life,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.040
we have to subtract however old you are right now, from 82.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.920
For the sake of this video, let's say you are 22 years old,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.580
as that is generally an age when a lot of people fully begin their careers.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.680
After subtracting that, at this point you now have 60 more potential years of life,

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.460
which equates to twenty one thousand nine hundred days,

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.940
or 525,600 hours still not too bad.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.940
But, if you get an average of eight hours of sleep per night,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.800
175,200 of those hours disappear,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.080
and you now have just less than what is equal to 40 years of waking life left.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.820
That brings your life expectancy down 20 years.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.820
That's kind of scary, but it gets a lot scarier if the following is true for you.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.260
A 2017 survey indicated that about 50% of US workers

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.680
described themselves as unsatisfied or unhappy in their job.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.540
In another 2017 study conducted worldwide,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.400
when asked anonymously, 85 percent of workers

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.660
admitted to disliking or hating their job.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.100
At such a large percentage, it is rather likely that one of these people might be you.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.020
Or perhaps you don't necessarily hate or dread your job,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.700
But you find yourself rarely ever enjoying it or feeling inspired by it,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.540
and are always wishing for the weekend,

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:47.309
always wanting the week to be over and for it to be Friday night.

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:51.629
If this is the case, and your life enjoyment is based almost entirely on the weekend,

00:01:51.629 --> 00:01:56.100
Let's see what happens when we subtract all the weekdays from your remaining 40 years of life.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.340
There is an average of 260 work days in a year, not including holidays.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.180
After subtracting those across your remaining 40 years,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.480
your number of days goes from 14,600, all the way down to 4200,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.860
leaving you with only about 11 years.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.699
But, if you figure you retire at an average age of around 63,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.940
You might say that you should get some of those days back.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:22.320
But if you also figure, that in the US, the chance of having a disability or mental impairment

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.259
is 68 percent for people over the age of 65,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.120
then this age isn't exactly a time of your life to hold out for,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.510
and claim as years filled with happiness and enjoyment.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.049
So in fact, I would argue that in terms of desirable life,

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:36.840
we should actually take at least a portion of those days away.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.700
First, let's give you them all back,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.740
but then let's consider that after the age of 65 as you grow older and older,

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.800
the likelihood of developing health conditions

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.200
or having existing health conditions worsen, only increases.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.140
So as a rough generalized average, across all the years after retirement,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.060
all the way up until death, let's say a person has somewhere around

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.169
two good, healthy, enjoyable days out of every seven.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.780
Now you are exactly right back where you were,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.980
with eleven years of remaining life.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:08.040
In living for the weekend, you went from what first sounded like a decent 60 years of remaining life,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.960
all the way down to only 11,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.500
and as an extra kicker what if you have weekends that aren't that good?

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.860
Weekends where you have to do things that you don't want to do,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.200
like house chores, yard work, dealing with annoying personal stuff

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.459
or even going into work to catch up on things.

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:24.659
That's at least another year or more, off of your remaining life.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.180
If you are 22, your waking life expectancy for life you want to live,

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.820
is now equivalent to around the age of 32,

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.900
regardless of your age, who you are, what you do,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.500
or the exact accuracy of these numbers to your life.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.180
If you live for the weekends, the point holds true.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.609
The idea that anyone would accept to live a life, where this amount of it is wished away,

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:48.839
where such a huge quantity of time is spent not wanting that time to happen,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.500
where such a small percentage is spent enjoying it and living in the moment.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.440
For anyone that has any sliver of hope in not living like this, It is borderline insanity to accept.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.260
Sure, there are responsibilities we must attend to in life.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.260
Sure, there are things we're going to have to do that we don't always want to do.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.140
Sure, every day of our job in career, can't always be fun or how we want it to be.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.580
But to work a job or be in a career or be at a company

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.100
that you don't enjoy or find fulfillment in, at least a majority of the time,

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.580
you're essentially signing away most of life.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:19.840
Life is extremely short.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.540
If you do out all of the math and consider every little trivial or self-maintenance-oriented thing we spend time doing,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.740
even if you love what you do for work and don't subtract all the weekdays from your remaining life,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.040
the time we have is still frighteningly short.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.780
So it truly is so important, that we do not give it away,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.680
that we are careful and conscious of what we exchange it for,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.060
that we do not let outside pressures from family friends or society

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:45.800
convince us to just give it away blindly and choose jobs, careers, companies or lifestyles

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.380
that we don't personally enjoy or resonate with,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.320
that we don't become easily distracted or persuaded by short-term glitz and glamour

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.500
that we know we don't really need, and that we try our best to avoid accepting anything less

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.440
or making big mistakes that force us to have to.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.860
If you feel like you are constantly wishing for the weekend,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.340
only for it to come and then in a blink of the eye,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.160
It ends, and you're back at the same starting point on Monday,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.380
waiting for the weekend all over again.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:13.640
If you feel like you are anywhere close to living a life where you dislike almost every day of what you do,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.980
throwing each day into the trash of wasted time,

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.080
perhaps you should spend some of your time

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.259
trying to figure out how to make sure you don't waste any more of it.

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:26.200
Thank you for watching.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.099
If you found enjoyment and value in this video, please give it a thumbs up.

00:05:31.099 --> 00:05:37.939
Share your thoughts in the comments below, and if you are not already, be sure to subscribe to our channel.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:45.720
We are constantly coming out with new videos to help you experience life with clarity and wonder.

